Signup:
1. Save username, password brcypt in database
2. create new pair public/private and save to database
3. createNew token

Login:
1. Check user existed in database
2. Create new pair public/private 
3. generate AT(1 hours) and RT(24 hours) using private key and save it to database. So that other system can use public key to decrypt the token

Logout:
1. deleted alltoken and usedToken

HandelerRefreshToken (create new pair when AT expired):
1. Check if RT used in database ==> If use mean that this RT is using by hacker ==> Force logout from all devices and deleted all token 
2. Check if previous pairToken is in database
3. Create new pair AccessToken and RefreshToken
4. Save RT in usedToken 

Authentication (In middle when calling any api):
nonce = encrypt (sessionID + apiTimeStamp + randomNumber)
Body: {
    "userId":1247591471344500736,
    "content":"91477f24dd92e11a920f7734cb23136c",
    "requestUri": "authService/generateFakeData",
    "nonce":"ECB47oRxLjXgDJyaa+vraA==",
    "signature":"E/d6nkAvCHCAXBs6rWcxNtSbRdkwZUdl03y6BO0Qvjw="
}

1. verify signature with content, nonce,...using secret_key
2. Check if apiTimeStamp is exceed from time
3. decrypt nonce to get session ID
4. Check if nonce is existed in redis, if existed then authen fail
5. Verify token and userId


cache (userInfo):
userID, username, email, password, status
sessionID: 40 bytes
nonce: 30 bytes
keyToken (publicKey, RT): 1KB

Considering using gzip vs snappy:

Gzip: compress more data, consume more cpu
Snappy: compress less data, consume less cpu